# Golden image / 2026-27

Current lifecycle is config-driven through `scripts/config/config.toml` and
`scripts/mct-vm.py`.

Normal classroom build:

1. Review `scripts/config/config.toml`, especially `[workflow]`, `[golden_image]` and `[run]`.
2. Review/update `scripts/config/rollout.csv` and run `./scripts/mct-vm.py generate-hosts` if identities changed.
3. Run `./scripts/mct-vm.py build-golden`.
4. Work through `doc/golden-manual-checklist.md` in the visible VM and shut it down cleanly.
5. Run `./scripts/mct-vm.py finalize-golden`.
6. Run `./scripts/mct-vm.py build-vms` (pilot with `[run].vms_include` first if desired).
7. Run `./scripts/mct-vm.py build-rollout-images`.
8. Run `./scripts/mct-vm.py stage-rollout`, then `python scripts\\mct-vm.py rollout` on the Windows teacher PC.

Intentional rollback uses `reset-golden`, `reset-finalized-golden`, `reset-vms`
or `reset-rollout-images`. Reset commands only remove/invalidate artifacts; they
never run the corresponding build automatically.

## Lockdown before first/second exam

- `scripts/config/rollout-lockdown.csv` must be created/reviewed for the actual exam seating/selection.
- `[lockdown].repo` must point to the exact clean local exam Git repository under ignored `repos/`.
- Pilot-test one lockdown VM end-to-end: local Git-bundle copy, final lockdown switch, old-generation removal, bootloader update, firewall validation and cold boot.
- Lockdown sudo is hardened: `student` is removed from `wheel`; only the exact password-protected firewall-stop command is allowed. Verify the generated per-VM fallback passwords before the exam.
- Git/HTTPS is the normal exam submission path; pilot-test create-repos -> build-vms origin -> grant -> git pub -> revoke end-to-end.

## Remaining cleanup

- `elevator=noop` is still injected by the Nixpkgs `virtualisation.hypervGuest` module.
  The kernel reports it as obsolete on 6.18. Do not globally force `boot.kernelParams`
  to empty just to silence it; remove it once Nixpkgs drops the parameter or when we
  replace the Hyper-V guest module locally without losing Hyper-V support.
